\section{Experiments}
\label{sec:experiments}

\subsection{Setup}

We fine-tune Qwen-2.5-7B-Instruct on 8K multi-turn conversations from the UltraChat training split, a dataset of naturalistic dialogues across diverse topics. LoRA adapters (rank 16, $\alpha$=32) are applied to query and value projection layers (\texttt{q\_proj}, \texttt{v\_proj}). The combined loss $\mathcal{L} = \mathcal{L}_\text{NTP} + \lambda_\text{GAR}\mathcal{L}_\text{GAR} + \lambda_\text{div}\mathcal{L}_\text{div}$ uses $\lambda_\text{GAR} = \lambda_\text{div} = 1.0$ with MSE target $\tau = 0.5$ (\S\ref{sec:loss}). We train for 3 epochs with AdamW (lr $= 2 \times 10^{-4}$, batch size 1, gradient accumulation 8, seed 42; ThreadGate additionally with seed 123); each variant requires approximately 6 hours on a single NVIDIA RTX PRO 6000 Blackwell Server Edition GPU (102\,GB). Full hyperparameters are provided in Appendix~\ref{sec:appendix-hyper}.

We evaluate five configurations that form a factorial design isolating four causal factors. \textbf{ThreadGate}: full system with 28 gate networks (Eq.~\ref{eq:gate}) and three-term loss. \textbf{+PCGrad}: ThreadGate with PCGrad \citep{yu2020pcgrad} projecting conflicting gradients (isolates gradient conflict). \textbf{Uniform Gate}: gates without turn embedding (isolates turn conditioning). \textbf{LoRA-only}: gates fixed at 1.0, trained with $\mathcal{L}_\text{NTP} + \mathcal{L}_\text{GAR}$ only (isolates gating mechanism). \textbf{Base}: pretrained model (isolates fine-tuning). All fine-tuned variants share training data, LoRA configuration, optimizer, and seed. We evaluate under \emph{IID} (same-distribution UltraChat text) and \emph{held-out} (50 non-overlapping conversations) teacher-forced protocols, reporting GAR-ours (last-token attention to goals, \S\ref{sec:background}), GAR-Dongre \citep{dongre2026attention} (all-token average), and PPL.

\subsection{Main Results}

Table~\ref{tab:four-way} presents the five-way comparison. The central finding is that all four fine-tuned variants converge to nearly identical performance. GAR-ours spans only 0.75 percentage points (pp) across ThreadGate (0.50), +PCGrad (0.50), Uniform Gate (0.51), and LoRA-only (0.50), and PPL is 2.78 for all four on IID evaluation. Neither per-layer gating (ThreadGate), elimination of gradient direction conflict (+PCGrad), nor content-only gating (Uniform Gate) provides any measurable benefit over LoRA-only fine-tuning.

The Base model confirms that LoRA fine-tuning is the operative variable: GAR-ours jumps from 0.01 to ${\sim}$0.50, while PPL improves from 4.23 to 2.78. Since all four variants share the same GAR loss ($\tau = 0.5$), convergence near the target is expected and does not demonstrate gating benefit. GAR-Dongre remains uniformly low (0.01--0.02) across all standard-trained conditions, consistent with \citet{dongre2026attention}; a preliminary scheduled sampling experiment breaks this pattern (\S\ref{sec:discussion}).

The teacher-forced GAR-ours of 0.50--0.51 stands in sharp contrast to autoregressive generation, where GAR-ours drops to 0.015--0.025 across all fine-tuned variants, a 20--33$\times$ gap. This gap is universal: it persists across all training strategies---gating, gradient conflict elimination, and LoRA-only fine-tuning alike. A second ThreadGate seed (123) confirms stability: TF GAR-ours $0.504\pm0.001$, AR GAR-ours $0.016\pm0.001$, gap ${\sim}$31$\times$ (range 30.5--32.1$\times$), with identical PPL. We analyze this distribution shift in \S\ref{sec:analysis}.

\begin{table*}[t]
\centering
\small
\caption{Five-way comparison on Qwen-2.5-7B-Instruct. IID evaluation uses teacher-forced UltraChat text; held-out uses 50 non-overlapping conversations. All four fine-tuned variants converge to within 0.75\,pp on GAR-ours; PPL is indistinguishable. GAR-O = GAR-ours; GAR-D = GAR-Dongre \citep{dongre2026attention}.}
\label{tab:four-way}
\begin{tabular}{l ccc ccc}
\toprule
& \multicolumn{3}{c}{\textbf{IID Evaluation}} & \multicolumn{3}{c}{\textbf{Held-out Evaluation}} \\
\cmidrule(lr){2-4} \cmidrule(lr){5-7}
\textbf{Configuration} & GAR-O & GAR-D & PPL & GAR-O & GAR-D & PPL \\
\midrule
Base              & 0.01 & 0.00 & 4.23 & 0.01 & 0.00 & 4.34 \\
ThreadGate        & 0.50 & 0.02 & 2.78 & 0.50 & 0.02 & 2.78 \\
\;+\,PCGrad       & 0.50 & 0.01 & 2.78 & 0.50 & 0.01 & 2.78 \\
Uniform Gate      & 0.51 & 0.01 & 2.78 & 0.51 & 0.01 & 2.78 \\
LoRA-only         & 0.50 & 0.01 & 2.78 & 0.50 & 0.01 & 2.78 \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\end{table*}

\begin{figure}[t]
  \centering
  \includegraphics[width=\columnwidth]{fig4_attention_heatmap.pdf}
  \caption{Per-turn GAR-ours under teacher-forced evaluation ($N$\,=\,45, $\pm$1\,std). Fine-tuned variants maintain GAR near $\tau$\,=\,0.5 with 7--8\% decay (T1$\to$T4); Base decays 80\%. T5 std inflated by truncated conversations. Despite this preservation, autoregressive GAR collapses to 0.015--0.025 for all variants (Table~\ref{tab:four-way}), distribution shift is the failure mode.}
  \label{fig:attention-heatmap}
\end{figure}

\subsection{Inference-Time Attention Steering}\label{subsec:steering}

The preceding sections established that all training-time interventions fail uniformly: exposure bias collapses autoregressive GAR regardless of gating or gradient management. We now test whether inference-time attention manipulation can recover GAR without retraining. We apply an additive bias $\beta$ to goal-token attention logits at every layer of the ThreadGate checkpoint during generation.

Figure~\ref{fig:steering}(a) presents initial results on $N$\,=\,5 ten-turn conversations. Low bias values ($\beta \in [0, 2]$) yield negligible GAR change (0.028\,$\to$\,0.062) with stable PPL (${\sim}$1.4). A sharp nonlinearity appears between $\beta$\,=\,2 and $\beta$\,=\,5: GAR increases ${\sim}$11$\times$ (0.062\,$\to$\,0.6795$\pm$0.020) while PPL rises only 35\% (1.41\,$\to$\,1.91). At $\beta$\,=\,10, GAR saturates at 0.959 but PPL degrades to 4.22.

$N$\,=\,50 conversations from a held-out evaluation set (39 with $\geq$3 turns, mean 3.6 user turns; Figure~\ref{fig:steering}b) confirm the qualitative steering pattern observed in the $N$\,=\,5 pilot (10 turns each), with consistent nonlinearity range and saturation behavior. A finer $\beta$ sweep localizes the nonlinearity: GAR jumps from 0.128$\pm$0.110 ($\beta$\,=\,2) to 0.556$\pm$0.060 ($\beta$\,=\,3), an 8.7$\times$ recovery from baseline with only 9\% PPL increase (1.43\,$\to$\,1.56), identifying $\beta$\,=\,3 as a practical operating point. The $\beta$\,=\,0 distribution is right-skewed (mean 0.064, median 0.031, std 0.081), driven by short conversations with high baseline GAR; absolute baselines therefore differ from the 10-turn $N$\,=\,5 evaluation (0.028).

These results provide constructive evidence that attention misallocation---not representation degradation---is the proximal cause of exposure-bias-induced GAR failure under the tested conditions (Qwen-2.5-7B, UltraChat, GAR-family losses): manually correcting attention via $\beta$ bias restores GAR, and inference-time intervention circumvents the exposure bias barrier.

\begin{figure}[t]
  \centering
  \includegraphics[width=\columnwidth]{fig5_attention_steering.pdf}
  \caption{Inference-time attention steering: additive bias $\beta$ applied to goal-token attention logits during autoregressive generation (error bars show $\pm$1\,std). (a)~Initial evaluation ($N$\,=\,5, 10-turn conversations): sharp increase at $\beta \in [2, 5]$, GAR ${\sim}$11$\times$ recovery with 35\% PPL rise. (b)~Larger-scale evaluation ($N$\,=\,50, 39 with $\geq$3 turns, mean 3.6 user turns) with finer $\beta$ sweep: the nonlinearity localizes between $\beta$\,=\,2 and $\beta$\,=\,3, identifying $\beta$\,=\,3 as a practical operating point (8.7$\times$ GAR recovery, 9\% PPL cost). High-$\beta$ results are consistent across sample sizes. Dotted line: unsteered teacher-forced baseline.}
  \label{fig:steering}
\end{figure}

\section{Introduction}\label{sec:introduction}

Multi-turn dialogue is the dominant mode of interaction with large language models (LLMs), and system prompts that specify task goals form the foundation of instruction following. As conversations grow longer, however, models progressively lose track of these goal specifications. \citet{dongre2026attention} quantified this degradation across 12 models: the Goal Attention Ratio (GAR), which measures how much attention the model allocates to goal tokens in the system prompt, declines by 27--48\% between early and mid-conversation turns. We replicate and extend this finding on Qwen-2.5-7B-Instruct, observing a 70.8\% relative decline in GAR from 0.0418 at turn~1 to 0.0122 at mid-conversation. We attribute the steeper decline partly to our use of last-token GAR, which captures summary-position attention more sensitively than the all-token average of \citet{dongre2026attention}, and partly to Qwen-2.5-7B's stronger instruction-following fidelity in early turns. This attention decay represents a systematic failure of multi-turn models to maintain task coherence, and it grows worse with each additional turn.

A striking aspect of this degradation is what it does \emph{not} affect. \citet{dongre2026attention} showed that goal-relevant information persists in the residual stream throughout the conversation: linear discriminant analysis probes recover goal content with AUC\,=\,0.98 even at late turns where attention to goal tokens has collapsed. The information is present in the hidden states, but the attention mechanism fails to route it toward the output computation. This dissociation between information availability and information utilization suggests a natural intervention: if the residual stream already carries goal information, a learnable gate on the residual pathway could amplify or attenuate layer contributions to recover goal attention. This reasoning motivates ThreadGate, which modulates each residual update with a content-and-turn-conditioned MLP gate: $x_{l+1} = x_l + g_l \cdot f_l(x_l)$, where $g_l$ is produced by a two-layer network conditioned on the hidden state and turn features, inspired by the residual scaling idea of ReZero \citep{bachlechner2021rezero}.

\begin{figure*}[t]
  \centering
  \includegraphics[width=\textwidth]{fig1_overview.pdf}
  \caption{Overview of ThreadGate and its failure mode. (Left) ThreadGate adds per-layer content-and-turn-conditioned MLP gates to the residual stream, trained jointly with an auxiliary GAR loss that targets goal attention. (Right) Under teacher-forced evaluation, GAR-ours reaches 0.50 across all fine-tuned variants; under autoregressive generation, it drops to 0.015--0.025, a 20--33$\times$ gap. The five-way comparison (ThreadGate, +PCGrad, Uniform Gate, LoRA-only, Base) shows that no intervention provides measurable benefit over LoRA-only fine-tuning.}
  \label{fig:overview}
\end{figure*}

ThreadGate combines per-layer MLP gate networks with a joint training objective: next-token prediction (NTP), an auxiliary GAR loss ($\tau = 0.5$), and a gate diversity loss (\S\ref{sec:method}). The final formulation emerged from seven iterations spanning four loss families; only MSE target with gate diversity converged stably. To diagnose the failure, we construct a five-way factorial comparison (ThreadGate, +PCGrad, Uniform Gate, LoRA-only, Base) where each pair isolates one causal factor (\S\ref{sec:experiments}). PCGrad \citep{yu2020pcgrad} serves a dual role: training strategy and causal exclusion tool that rules out gradient direction conflict.

Training succeeds: teacher-forced GAR-ours reaches 0.50 across all fine-tuned variants. The factorial comparison reveals that the failure is specific to autoregressive generation, where GAR-ours drops to 0.015--0.025, a 20--33$\times$ gap that persists across all training strategies. All four fine-tuned variants achieve indistinguishable performance (0.75\,pp spread on GAR-ours, identical PPL at 2.78); the gating contrast confirms that gates contribute zero measurable benefit. The PCGrad causal exclusion eliminates gradient direction conflict without altering any outcome, establishing that conflict is neither necessary nor sufficient for the failure. These four contrasts converge on a single root cause: exposure bias \citep{bengio2015scheduled}, where attention patterns optimized under teacher-forced prefixes do not transfer to model-generated text (Figure~\ref{fig:overview}). An inference-time steering experiment provides constructive evidence that attention misallocation is the proximal cause: additive attention bias recovers autoregressive GAR from 0.064 to 0.727, bypassing the exposure bias barrier (\S\ref{subsec:steering}). A preliminary scheduled sampling experiment confirms that the gap is also mitigable at training time: GAR-ours increases from 0.016 to 0.318 (20$\times$), compressing the ${\sim}$31$\times$ gap to 1.57$\times$ with no PPL cost (single seed; \S\ref{sec:discussion}).

Our contributions: (1)~a systematic causal diagnostic framework for analyzing auxiliary objective failures in multi-turn dialogue, comprising a five-way factorial comparison that isolates four causal factors (turn conditioning, gating mechanism, gradient conflict, and fine-tuning), PCGrad-based causal exclusion via gradient surgery \citep{yu2020pcgrad}, and per-layer gradient conflict quantification; this design pattern is in principle applicable to other auxiliary objective analyses (\S\ref{sec:method}, \S\ref{sec:experiments}, \S\ref{sec:analysis}); (2)~using this framework, the first empirical characterization of how exposure bias selectively destroys auxiliary attention loss generalization (20--33$\times$ gap) while leaving NTP robust, with evidence that token-level self-recovery \citep{he2019exposure} does not extend to auxiliary objectives, a preliminary demonstration that scheduled sampling compresses this gap from ${\sim}$31$\times$ to 1.57$\times$ (single seed), and cross-model confirmation on Llama-3.1-8B (27$\times$ gap); (3)~an inference-time steering experiment: additive attention bias at $\beta$\,=\,5 recovers autoregressive GAR from 0.064 to 0.727, providing constructive evidence that attention misallocation---not representation degradation---is the proximal cause under the tested conditions (Qwen-2.5-7B and Llama-3.1-8B, UltraChat, GAR-family losses; \S\ref{subsec:steering}); (4)~a principled negative baseline for future attention steering research: ThreadGate with seven loss formulations and four training configurations ruled out (\S\ref{sec:method}, \S\ref{sec:experiments}).

